WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:02.350
OK.

00:00:03.210 --> 00:00:04.386
We're

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.889
now at one hundred and twenty one

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:09.556
attendees, it's wonderful turnout.

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.220
Thanks to everyone for coming.

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:12.250
I'll start again.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:14.020
My name is Stephen Naron.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:15.329
I'm the director of the Fortunoff Video

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:17.309
Archive for Holocaust Testimonies

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.090
here at Yale.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.247
Tonight, the Fortunoff Archive is delighted

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.489
to present the work of Zisl Slepovitch and

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.204
his ensemble featuring Sasha Lurje.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.139
The songs you're about to hear are taken from

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.209
the second volume of recorded works based on

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.520
the testimonies from the archive.

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:34.229
After the concert, you can learn more about

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.420
this ongoing project and listen to both

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:38.250
volumes one and two, and watch

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.429
excerpts from the testimonies at the

00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:42.665
project's accompanying website,

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:44.660
songsfromtestimonies.org.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.660
Before we begin, I'd like to thank

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.949
Adam Graves from the Institute for Sacred

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.011
Music and Rabbi Jason Rubinstein at the

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:54.629
Joseph Slifka Center for Jewish Life

00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:57.029
at Yale, for their generous support of this

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:58.030
concert.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.180
I'd like to give you just a few,

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.659
a few bits of information about the Fortunoff

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:04.660
Video Archive.

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:06.877
The archive began taping testimonies in New

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:08.159
Haven in 1979.

00:01:08.790 --> 00:01:10.589
At the time it was called the Holocaust

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.230
Survivors Film Project, or HSFP,

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.681
and it was a local grassroots effort by

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:17.849
survivors and supporters in the New Haven

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:18.850
area.

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.339
It was the brainchild of Dr. Dori Laub,

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:23.173
himself a survivor from Czernowitz, a

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.415
psychiatrist and analyst, and Laura

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.989
Vlock, a television journalist at Channel

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:28.990
Eight.

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.599
They had the support of the New Haven

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:32.310
survivor community from the very beginning,

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.949
particularly in the form of a man named

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.170
William Rosenberg, who was a survivor from

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:38.830
Czestochowa, Poland.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.437
And Willy became the president of the HSFP,

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:43.949
organized, helped organize the tapings,

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:46.008
raising funds for the tapings, and holding

00:01:46.260 --> 00:01:47.260
meetings in his home.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.316
The collection came to Yale in 1981,

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.529
thanks to the work of Professor Geoffrey

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.441
Hartman. Geoffrey too was a survivor of

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.909
Nazi occupied Europe, having fled Frankfurt

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:58.800
on a Kindertransport for the UK.

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.499
And his wife, Renee was one of the first four

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.540
survivors taped by the Holocaust

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:05.740
Survivors Film Project in 1979.

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:09.429
Geoffrey helped find a home for the

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.260
collection at Manuscripts and Archives, at

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.359
Sterling Memorial Library, and under his

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:15.339
watch as faculty advisor, it grew to become

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.559
an internationally renowned collection

00:02:17.860 --> 00:02:19.749
that over the years has influenced the way

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:21.190
the history of the Holocaust is studied,

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:22.340
written and taught.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:25.780
Now the collection contains something like

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:28.740
4,400 testimonies, 12,000 hours of recorded

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.460
material, in over a dozen languages

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.620
recorded over the last 40 years, in over

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:34.940
a dozen countries.

00:02:35.740 --> 00:02:37.839
Each testimony in the collection is really a

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:40.089
world unto itself, functioning almost like an

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.429
audio visual autobiography because

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:44.243
we use an open-ended methodology that

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.858
allows for a great deal of spontaneity and

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:48.789
freedom for the individual to tell their

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:49.880
story the way they desire.

00:02:50.410 --> 00:02:52.899
Because of this, it's no wonder that song

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.180
and poetry appear in so many testimonies.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.559
Historian Timothy Snyder, the archives

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.869
faculty advisor, has said that video

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:03.119
testimony, like works of art, have a special

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.227
ability to cross the membrane between death

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.310
and life, between past and present.

00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:10.098
The singing of these songs can't happen in

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:11.760
a written source the same way.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.649
They can only happen in an audio or

00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:15.040
audiovisual source.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:17.999
The collection, preservation and presentation

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.160
of musical performances like these

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:22.648
also places this project in a long line of

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.703
venerable ethnomusicological projects

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.480
that stretch back to the early 20th century

00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.450
when individuals like S.

00:03:29.454 --> 00:03:31.350
Ansky went on expeditions to record

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.659
the musical heritage of a Jewish society that

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:35.459
was disappearing due to modernization,

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.009
urbanization and political turmoil.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:40.320
Ansky called this work salvage ethnography.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:42.845
This impetus to collect and record,

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.269
this goal of salvaging what was left,

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.069
continued through the war and into the

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.380
postwar period following the Holocaust,

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:51.855
with recordings of songs by David Boder in DP

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.239
camps, and later the work of the Fortunoff

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:55.240
Video Archive.

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:58.217
My point is simply, we are not the first to

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.859
recognize the significance of musical

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.529
expression as a means for understanding

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.588
the past, and it most certainly applies to

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:06.969
the understanding of the multilingual,

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:09.399
multicultural world where European Jews lived

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:11.169
and died during the Holocaust.

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.630
And with that, I would like to introduce Zisl

00:04:13.810 --> 00:04:16.174
Slepovitch, the Fortunoff Video Archive's

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:17.450
Musician-in-residence.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:20.300
Thank you, Stephen.

00:04:21.270 --> 00:04:23.729
Songs From Testimonies in the Fortunoff Video

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.039
Archive is a project that explores

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:28.260
and artistically presents music

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.574
and poetic pieces performed by survivors in

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.500
testimonies given to the Archive.

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:35.961
Some of the songs were from the pre-war

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:38.819
period. Some of them gained new meaning

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:40.241
during the Holocaust, playing

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.240
many different roles.

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.700
But often songs worked to sustain

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:46.742
people psychologically in unimaginable

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.919
conditions when even the

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:51.665
very act of singing a song required

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:53.650
enormous effort.

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.121
Some of the songs are not idiomatically

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:57.878
Jewish. They might be songs from

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:01.109
the past that acquired new topical lyrics.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.289
There are songs of struggle and resistance,

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.319
many songs that are satirical in nature,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.590
songs documenting people suffering,

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.779
songs naming the traitors and

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:13.919
collaborationists.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.026
They are even overtly antisemitic

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.169
songs that survivors nevertheless chose

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:20.170
to perform.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:23.219
This project began several years ago

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.274
when Dr. Sarah Garibova, former

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:27.415
Fortunoff Video Archive

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:31.469
Haartman Fellow, began research on the

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:32.744
testimony of Liubov' K., a

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:35.542
survivor of the slave labor camp in

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:38.069
Zvenigorodka, Ukraine.

00:05:38.940 --> 00:05:41.309
In her testimony, Liubov' recounts

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:42.899
several songs and poems.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:46.140
Later, Steven Naron, the Archive's director,

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.054
invited me to transcribe and perform

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:51.269
the songs, and the project has grown from

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:52.270
there.

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.404
When I started working on this collection in

00:05:54.405 --> 00:05:57.029
2018, I only had a sense

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:58.514
of the richness, depth and

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:02.490
breadth of the material I would be examining.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:05.280
The more I listen to the testimonies,

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:07.374
the more I found myself placing them

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.919
in many different contexts: European,

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:12.540
Soviet, Jewish, musicological,

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:16.609
ethnographical, and so on.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.398
When Stephen asked me to record the songs,

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.406
it became obvious to me that I had

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:23.910
to contract an ensemble,

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:27.149
a vocalist and instrumentalists, musicians

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:29.729
that would be the right fit for this

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:31.300
very challenging material.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:33.632
Among those challenges, to name just a

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.719
few, are extremely wide range

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.209
of musical styles and many

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.189
languages in which the vocalists would have

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:43.649
to convince-convincingly perform.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.419
In the two volumes of Songs From Testimonies,

00:06:47.430 --> 00:06:49.859
Sasha Lurje sings Yiddish,

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:51.377
Hebrew, Polish, German,

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:54.465
Russian, mixed Yiddish/German

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:56.500
and French.

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:58.919
The two volumes have been the most

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.169
stylistically and linguistically diverse

00:07:01.170 --> 00:07:03.779
albums that I have ever recorded

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:04.780
or produced.

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:07.041
To ensure that each song is represented

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:10.030
in a historically authentic manner.

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:12.899
A great deal of multidisciplinary research

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:14.468
is required even before the song

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.039
is transcribed and arranged.

00:07:17.850 --> 00:07:19.839
From the very beginning of this project, I

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:22.109
have been very fortunate to work with Joshua

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:24.070
Camp, who plays the accordion, piano and

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:26.755
the guitar, with bassist Dmitri

00:07:26.914 --> 00:07:29.030
Ishenko, violinist Craig Judelman,

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:30.860
and vocalist Sasha Lurje.

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:33.959
Mariana Slepovitch appears on two

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.660
songs in Volume Two, on the tsymbaly,

00:07:36.570 --> 00:07:38.609
a Belarusian string instrument

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:40.859
similar to the hammered dulcimer.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:43.732
As time passes, we obviously are moved

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.580
farther away from the origins of the

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:49.159
source material, but our hope

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:51.409
is that anyone listening to these songs will

00:07:51.410 --> 00:07:52.782
be driven to also learn more

00:07:53.750 --> 00:07:55.909
about the survivors who sang them.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:59.089
The songs represent many things, including

00:07:59.090 --> 00:08:01.879
stories of loss and survival,

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:04.370
stories that are still important to hear

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:07.039
today when we are experiencing

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:08.951
a new upheaval of hateful, chauvinistic

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:11.969
ideas and ideologists and

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:13.350
ideologies.

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:16.459
Antisemitism itself, sadly, is hardly a

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:17.460
thing of the past.

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:19.969
Music, in a way different from a verbal

00:08:19.970 --> 00:08:22.550
story, has the power to educate,

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:24.589
to challenge people's thinking.

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:27.380
Although I hope I'm not being naive,

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:30.289
I do sincerely hope that this

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:32.839
will be true for these recordings as well.

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:37.177
We will begin our program tonight

00:08:37.989 --> 00:08:39.390
with the song, "In Dinaverke."

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:42.596
Lily M. was born in 1924

00:08:44.389 --> 00:08:46.670
in Vilna, now Vilnius, Lithuania,

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:48.630
then Poland.

00:08:49.370 --> 00:08:51.889
In her testimony, Lily recalls growing

00:08:51.890 --> 00:08:54.229
up in an assimilated, affluent

00:08:54.230 --> 00:08:55.455
home, as well as the rise

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:58.669
of antisemitism from

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:00.390
1935.

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:03.579
At some point, Lily was transported

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:05.770
to the external slave labor camp

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:07.836
Dünawerke, Dinaverke in

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:10.490
Yiddish, in Kaiserwald, in the outskirts

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:12.900
of Riga, Latvia.

00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:16.509
According to Lily's testimony, her friend

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:18.999
Tosia had escaped

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:21.249
with her parents from Lodz to Vilna in

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:23.413
1939, and composed and sang

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:25.400
this song.

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:28.626
Lily characterized Tosia as

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:30.420
a very talented poet.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.630
Tosia did not survive the war.

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:36.260
We believed she may have died

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:39.020
of typhus in Germany around the time

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:42.340
of the liberation in 1945.

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:12.740
In Dinaverke,

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:16.980
By the river, by the woods,

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:20.500
There are Jews

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:24.820
from all over the world.

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:29.370
They all are working hard

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:33.170
out in the cold.

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:37.100
Outside, it’s still dark

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:41.100
The bell is calling to work.

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:44.820
Rows of people are lining up,

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:48.380
The wind is whispering.

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:52.180
The Jews are convicted

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:56.740
But for whose sins?

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:00.380
The day goes by swiftly.

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:04.540
The sun has long set.

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:08.500
They return together

00:11:09.100 --> 00:11:12.860
Walking slowly

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:17.540
From the whole day of work

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:21.220
They are exhausted.

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:57.700
But Jews, be brave,

00:11:57.980 --> 00:12:01.260
The time shall come,

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:05.180
And we shall work

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:08.860
for ourselve in our own land!

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:12.820
And we will work

00:12:13.300 --> 00:12:17.580
For ourselves in our own land!

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:48.140
I would like to turn to the musicians

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:51.650
and ask them to speak about their experiences

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:54.419
working with this material on both albums.

00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:57.229
Joshua Camp was the first one of

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.460
this band who encountered Songs

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:00.860
with--From Testimonies.

00:13:02.600 --> 00:13:03.600
Thank you.

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:07.609
Yeah, I was involved from the very

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:10.249
first duo performances, and

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:12.320
it's been lovely to watch this project grow

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:14.429
and expand to what it is today.

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:18.079
Zisl uses the term "creative reconstruction"

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:19.909
for what we do. And I think that's a great

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:22.009
description of

00:13:22.490 --> 00:13:24.597
how we as musicians contribute to a project

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:25.920
like this.

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:29.119
Music performance is an ephemeral act,

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:30.919
but through that act, we can transform

00:13:30.920 --> 00:13:32.684
something that is perceived as being

00:13:33.230 --> 00:13:35.629
in the past and bring it to the

00:13:35.630 --> 00:13:36.630
here and now.

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:39.603
In our current situation, where we as

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:42.049
musicians cannot perform together in front of

00:13:42.050 --> 00:13:44.059
an audience, this project has been a much

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:46.910
needed reminder, at least for me,

00:13:47.270 --> 00:13:49.879
of the value of music and music performance,

00:13:50.150 --> 00:13:52.609
and how it can connect us with our past

00:13:52.740 --> 00:13:54.540
and our shared humanity.

00:13:57.860 --> 00:13:59.660
"A Dermonung Funem Appellplatz,"

00:14:00.280 --> 00:14:02.019
"A Memory of the Roll Call."

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:03.800
Moshe B.

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:05.820
was born in 1926 in Rymanów,

00:14:06.520 --> 00:14:09.129
Poland, near the Czechoslovakian border.

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:12.030
After imprisonment in several camps, Moshe

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:14.530
survived the death march to Bergen-Belsen,

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:17.559
where he was then liberated by the British

00:14:17.560 --> 00:14:18.560
troops.

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:21.489
In his testimony, Moshe remembers the grim

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:23.440
experience of the daily roll call,

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:25.839
"appellplatz" in German at

00:14:25.930 --> 00:14:27.502
one of the concentration camps.

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:30.910
The commandant would order everyone to take

00:14:30.940 --> 00:14:32.590
hats off, "Mützen ab,"

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:35.700
and to sing, "Singen!"

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:38.649
Moshe pays tribute to one of

00:14:38.650 --> 00:14:41.200
his musically talented fellow prisoners

00:14:41.440 --> 00:14:42.959
whose name remains unknown, who

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:46.180
sang this song during the roll call.

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:54.009
My heart is aching,

00:14:55.210 --> 00:14:57.940
but what does my cry help,

00:14:58.820 --> 00:15:01.460
If no one hears me?

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:04.860
They only chase me,

00:15:04.950 --> 00:15:08.380
I feel beaten up.

00:15:08.580 --> 00:15:12.100
I have no energy to think.

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:14.900
A human cannot understand,

00:15:15.750 --> 00:15:17.860
when tossing a round coin,

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:20.260
On which side it will fall.

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:23.500
One always aspires

00:15:24.020 --> 00:15:26.340
To enjoy life,

00:15:27.340 --> 00:15:30.340
And have joy in one’s heart.

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:44.300
We will now hear the band perform

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:45.980
the song.

00:15:58.340 --> 00:16:00.180
My heart is aching,

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:02.980
How can my cries help,

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:06.460
If no one hears me?

00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:09.820
They keep chasing me,

00:16:10.100 --> 00:16:12.460
I feel beaten up.

00:16:12.820 --> 00:16:16.020
I have no energy left to think.

00:16:16.620 --> 00:16:18.900
No one can influence

00:16:19.180 --> 00:16:21.060
Tossing a round coin,

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:24.980
On which side it falls.

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:27.940
One always aspires

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:30.180
To enjoy life,

00:16:30.700 --> 00:16:33.180
To keep joy in one’s heart.

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:39.180
Man, you are foolish.

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:41.300
Don’t forget,

00:16:43.460 --> 00:16:46.260
That all your cries

00:16:46.780 --> 00:16:48.540
Are just earth and clay.

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:51.900
Stuffing your wallet,

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:55.380
You think you’re so smart!

00:16:56.380 --> 00:16:59.140
Don't forget you come naked,

00:17:00.220 --> 00:17:02.940
And naked you return.

00:17:04.720 --> 00:17:08.340
You could be rich as Korah.

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:11.340
And bathe in luck,

00:17:13.820 --> 00:17:17.180
But here you're just a guest

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:19.700
When you get called back.

00:17:20.060 --> 00:17:22.580
Stuffing your wallet

00:17:22.860 --> 00:17:26.060
You think you’re so smart!

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:29.860
Don't forget you come naked,

00:17:30.740 --> 00:17:33.340
And naked you return.

00:18:12.820 --> 00:18:16.100
Man, you are foolish.

00:18:16.380 --> 00:18:18.860
Don’t forget,

00:18:20.060 --> 00:18:22.700
That all your cries

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:24.820
Are just earth and clay.

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:28.660
Stuffing your wallet

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:32.060
You think you’re so smart.

00:18:32.740 --> 00:18:35.460
Don't forget you come naked,

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:38.900
And naked you return.

00:18:41.500 --> 00:18:44.700
You could be rich as Korah.

00:18:45.340 --> 00:18:47.980
And bathe in luck,

00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:53.420
But you're just a visitor here

00:18:54.300 --> 00:18:56.700
When you get called back.

00:18:56.940 --> 00:18:58.780
Stuffing your wallet,

00:18:59.020 --> 00:19:02.380
You think you’re so smart.

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:06.020
Don't forget you come naked,

00:19:07.220 --> 00:19:09.700
And naked you return.

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:13.100
Don't forget you come naked,

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:15.820
And naked you return.

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:24.169
Sasha Lurje is the lead vocalist

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:25.170
of this project.

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:27.262
She's truly a creative force, standing

00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:30.349
behind many of the ideas you will hear

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:31.399
in this new program.

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:34.325
Sasha, I would like to ask you to share a few

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:36.829
words about your work in this project.

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:39.580
Thank you, Zisl.

00:19:42.310 --> 00:19:44.749
Well, it definitely has been--

00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:49.480
am I still there?

00:19:50.570 --> 00:19:51.780
Just double checking.

00:19:52.540 --> 00:19:53.540
OK.

00:19:54.220 --> 00:19:55.220
All right.

00:19:55.270 --> 00:19:57.639
So, it definitely has

00:19:57.640 --> 00:20:00.399
been a privilege, a great privilege

00:20:00.400 --> 00:20:03.099
to work with this project

00:20:03.100 --> 00:20:04.139
for many reasons.

00:20:04.220 --> 00:20:05.220
And first of all, of

00:20:06.550 --> 00:20:08.290
course, is the

00:20:09.010 --> 00:20:11.379
group of musicians and

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:13.869
artists and scholars that

00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:15.640
I got to work with on this.

00:20:17.230 --> 00:20:18.230
As a singer, I feel

00:20:19.660 --> 00:20:21.669
that learning the songs from real people,

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:24.669
even on recordings

00:20:24.670 --> 00:20:26.949
and video, brings a different level of

00:20:26.950 --> 00:20:28.812
connection to their stories, and their

00:20:29.230 --> 00:20:32.050
personal lives and personal voices.

00:20:33.560 --> 00:20:35.079
And by exploring the wide range

00:20:36.160 --> 00:20:39.219
of repertoire, styles and languages

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:42.610
that the people that were interviewed

00:20:43.210 --> 00:20:45.520
in these testimonies

00:20:45.550 --> 00:20:47.949
were fluent in, we get

00:20:47.950 --> 00:20:49.371
a distinct picture of what it

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:52.689
meant to be a Jewish person

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:55.350
before before World War II.

00:20:57.260 --> 00:20:58.926
Jewish people were integrated into

00:20:59.740 --> 00:21:01.308
the society around them and were

00:21:02.020 --> 00:21:03.539
polyglot not only in languages,

00:21:04.330 --> 00:21:07.329
but also in styles of music, literature,

00:21:07.330 --> 00:21:10.029
art and all the various

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:11.320
cultures around them.

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:14.649
Going between Yiddish, German, Polish,

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:17.070
Russian or even other languages

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:19.199
wasn't a special, unique thing.

00:21:19.210 --> 00:21:21.189
It was just part of how

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:22.780
the world functioned.

00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:26.709
And I get to have this unique

00:21:26.710 --> 00:21:28.327
chance to try out these different

00:21:29.290 --> 00:21:32.169
voices, cultures and styles

00:21:32.770 --> 00:21:34.142
and feel them through my own

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:36.170
voice.

00:21:37.490 --> 00:21:39.009
It definitely has been a deeply

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:41.368
interesting and moving challenge

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:43.200
to take on.

00:21:43.610 --> 00:21:44.933
And by bringing these songs

00:21:46.280 --> 00:21:47.815
to concerts

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:51.529
and recording them, would bring

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:53.245
new life to the stories that people

00:21:53.810 --> 00:21:55.380
shared in their testimonies.

00:21:55.760 --> 00:21:56.838
We bring these stories

00:21:58.370 --> 00:22:00.770
to people and it's so important that

00:22:00.920 --> 00:22:03.319
these stories and sounds

00:22:03.320 --> 00:22:04.320
get heard.

00:22:05.690 --> 00:22:07.356
And as Zisl has already mentioned,

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:10.760
it's sad to know that

00:22:11.750 --> 00:22:14.989
a lot of these situations,

00:22:14.990 --> 00:22:17.750
a lot of these stories are topical

00:22:17.900 --> 00:22:18.900
today.

00:22:19.490 --> 00:22:21.919
But learning this,

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:24.439
performing this, listening to this,

00:22:24.440 --> 00:22:26.929
helps us remember.

00:22:29.180 --> 00:22:30.258
Share our feelings and

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:33.619
experiences and live

00:22:34.070 --> 00:22:36.439
through the experiences of

00:22:36.440 --> 00:22:38.689
people that were interviewed, and

00:22:39.440 --> 00:22:41.809
I hope that it will help

00:22:41.810 --> 00:22:44.300
us learn how to be a better human.

00:22:47.150 --> 00:22:48.150
Thank you, Sasha.

00:22:49.920 --> 00:22:50.920
Kiddush Hashem.

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:54.020
This is another song

00:22:54.680 --> 00:22:55.797
remembered by Moshe B.

00:22:57.200 --> 00:23:00.079
He sang it as a tribute to Zhelazny,

00:23:00.920 --> 00:23:03.169
possibly a former synagogue cantor, a

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:05.582
talented singer in Moshe's own words.

00:23:06.500 --> 00:23:08.929
While one, can one can define this piece as

00:23:08.930 --> 00:23:11.209
a ballad due to his great dramatic

00:23:11.210 --> 00:23:14.029
intensity, it can be also categorized

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:15.410
as an operatic scene.

00:23:16.430 --> 00:23:17.704
Such songs would typically

00:23:18.770 --> 00:23:20.590
be performed in cantors' concerts on motz'ei

00:23:20.880 --> 00:23:22.549
Shabes, after Shabbat.

00:23:23.240 --> 00:23:26.000
In this piece, the dramatis personae

00:23:26.180 --> 00:23:28.302
include the congregation, der gantser

00:23:28.500 --> 00:23:29.578
oylom; the old Cantor,

00:23:30.770 --> 00:23:33.169
der alter khazn; the evil

00:23:33.170 --> 00:23:35.318
Gentile Sailor, der proster matros;

00:23:36.950 --> 00:23:38.471
the old rabbi, der alter rov;

00:23:40.010 --> 00:23:42.223
and an old blind Jew, a

00:23:42.565 --> 00:23:45.079
yid a zokn a blinder, who ultimately

00:23:45.080 --> 00:23:47.359
sacrifices his life to save the

00:23:47.360 --> 00:23:48.360
community.

00:23:48.590 --> 00:23:50.501
This is one of a series of dramatic and

00:23:50.810 --> 00:23:53.089
dramatized Ashkenazic Jewish ballads

00:23:53.330 --> 00:23:55.789
that are not coincidentally set on the eve

00:23:55.790 --> 00:23:58.306
of Yom Kippur, the Day of Atonement.

00:23:59.150 --> 00:24:01.639
This theatrical song performed by

00:24:01.640 --> 00:24:03.649
Moshe in a manner almost identical to our

00:24:04.070 --> 00:24:06.799
recording, cuts a nearly operatic scene

00:24:06.920 --> 00:24:08.929
that belongs to the Eastern European Jewish

00:24:08.930 --> 00:24:11.719
musical tradition, while also

00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:13.645
resonating with a kind of Brechtian

00:24:14.570 --> 00:24:16.530
theater along the lines of what we would

00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:18.380
hear from Kurt Weill.

00:24:32.800 --> 00:24:33.939
The sun has set,

00:24:36.290 --> 00:24:39.380
The day has passed.

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:43.740
The night of Kol Nidre has come

00:24:45.020 --> 00:24:49.780
And with it a great deal of awe.

00:24:50.900 --> 00:24:54.460
The small beys-medresh is like a cemetery.

00:24:54.700 --> 00:25:00.420
The whole congregation has gathered there,

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:05.460
In the old, broken synagogue

00:25:07.700 --> 00:25:13.020
Rivers of tears stream from their eyes

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:20.700
When the old cantor recites

00:25:22.620 --> 00:25:24.980
Suddenly, at that very moment,

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:26.660
Someone stands up and screams:

00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:28.980
One, two, three, four!

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:30.820
The door opens,

00:25:31.100 --> 00:25:33.860
A rough sailor walks in.

00:25:34.420 --> 00:25:36.460
Ha-ha-ha!

00:25:37.220 --> 00:25:40.900
How filthy it is in here!

00:25:41.420 --> 00:25:44.540
Who is in charge of this place?

00:25:47.820 --> 00:25:50.860
The old rabbi

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:54.500
takes several heavy steps,

00:25:54.900 --> 00:25:59.220
And wipes the tears from his eyes.

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:01.220
He asks:

00:26:01.500 --> 00:26:06.380
How can I help you, Sir?’

00:26:07.580 --> 00:26:11.260
I have hidden a spy in this synagogue!

00:26:11.780 --> 00:26:13.260
If I don’t find the spy –

00:26:13.460 --> 00:26:14.980
I will burn down this synagogue

00:26:15.260 --> 00:26:17.420
Together with all you Jews!

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:25.860
Perhaps there is a mistake, noble sir.

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:30.260
Can you really murder the whole Jewish community?

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:33.180
A spy?

00:26:33.780 --> 00:26:38.540
Today, on this holy night?

00:26:39.220 --> 00:26:40.380
Quiet!

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:44.620
Friends, it's me

00:26:45.500 --> 00:26:48.100
I want to die.

00:26:49.860 --> 00:26:54.420
So yells out a Jew, an old, blind one.

00:26:54.700 --> 00:26:57.260
I will die for you,

00:26:57.460 --> 00:26:59.660
in sanctification of the Holy Name

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:03.740
I’ll go straight to Heaven,

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:09.020
shot dead without mercy.

00:27:09.500 --> 00:27:13.500
That's a Jewish hero!

00:27:14.660 --> 00:27:18.140
The Jewish people have never seen

00:27:18.420 --> 00:27:22.180
such a hero before.

00:27:25.980 --> 00:27:30.340
And the congregation at the synagogue

00:27:30.540 --> 00:27:34.140
in the quiet morning

00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:39.380
cries and weeps,

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:44.340
as the old cantor recites

00:27:46.940 --> 00:27:54.780
Kol Nidre!

00:28:03.060 --> 00:28:04.860
Joseph W.

00:28:04.920 --> 00:28:07.900
was born in Lodz, Poland,

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:10.780
in 1929,

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:13.620
where, when Nazi occupation began,

00:28:14.490 --> 00:28:17.009
he and his family were locked

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:18.010
in the ghetto.

00:28:18.706 --> 00:28:19.706
One

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:23.339
of Joseph's brightest memories

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:25.153
was Jankele Herszkowicz, who composed

00:28:26.280 --> 00:28:28.349
many songs in the ghetto

00:28:28.560 --> 00:28:31.319
and was famous as the ghetto

00:28:31.320 --> 00:28:32.320
troubadour.

00:28:35.769 --> 00:28:37.876
Shtubuneltsto, a Yiddishized version of the

00:28:38.190 --> 00:28:39.190
German Stubeältester,

00:28:41.220 --> 00:28:43.529
the original meaning of the term, was

00:28:43.530 --> 00:28:45.569
a senior soldier

00:28:46.050 --> 00:28:47.999
in the barrack room

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:51.564
in the concentration and slave labor

00:28:52.410 --> 00:28:53.410
camps.

00:28:53.640 --> 00:28:54.754
The word meant Kapo,

00:28:56.430 --> 00:28:58.949
a prisoner functionary assigned

00:28:58.950 --> 00:29:01.349
by the SS guards to supervise

00:29:01.350 --> 00:29:03.359
forced labor or

00:29:03.750 --> 00:29:05.940
carry out administrative tasks.

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:09.410
Stubeältester, the room

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:12.210
leader, was the lowest rank.

00:29:12.780 --> 00:29:14.090
The next one was Blockältester,

00:29:15.270 --> 00:29:17.120
referred to in this song as Blokhovo,

00:29:19.410 --> 00:29:21.482
the block or the barracks

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:22.840
senior.

00:29:23.730 --> 00:29:26.297
The highest position of a Jew

00:29:28.020 --> 00:29:30.210
in a camp would be a

00:29:30.450 --> 00:29:31.450
kapo, lagerältester,

00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:35.789
the camp senior.

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:38.760
The discovery of the

00:29:40.160 --> 00:29:41.160
version of Shtubuneltso

00:29:43.100 --> 00:29:46.219
in Joseph's testimony is particularly

00:29:46.220 --> 00:29:47.220
important as the

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:49.845
survivors themselves seem

00:29:51.230 --> 00:29:52.847
to want to forget this repertoire

00:29:53.690 --> 00:29:55.940
and did not sing this song

00:29:56.630 --> 00:29:59.349
in the postwar period,

00:29:59.540 --> 00:30:00.779
as far as we know.

00:30:01.460 --> 00:30:04.099
So let's hear a clip

00:30:04.100 --> 00:30:06.199
from Joseph's testimony.

00:30:10.950 --> 00:30:14.620
Shtubuneltsto, oh you
have your preferences,

00:30:14.860 --> 00:30:17.780
You reprimand one, and you slap the other.

00:30:18.300 --> 00:30:19.420
Don’t start yelling,

00:30:19.740 --> 00:30:21.140
there’s nothing to eat.

00:30:21.460 --> 00:30:23.500
As father said, “Ah! Everyone’s a hustler.”

00:30:26.130 --> 00:30:29.500
Shtubuneltso conveys the grim reality

00:30:29.780 --> 00:30:31.700
of the unbearable conditions at the camp.

00:30:32.060 --> 00:30:34.279
The Yiddish lyrics carry a strong influence

00:30:34.280 --> 00:30:36.620
of German as well as camp slang.

00:30:37.130 --> 00:30:39.199
It would be hard, if not impossible, to

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:41.690
properly transcribe and understand the song,

00:30:41.870 --> 00:30:43.879
if not for the good chance that we received

00:30:43.880 --> 00:30:45.882
the assistance of Samuel Norich,

00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:47.869
the publisher of the Forward.

00:30:48.470 --> 00:30:49.597
Norich was born in 1947

00:30:50.810 --> 00:30:53.179
in a DP camp in Germany, where such

00:30:53.180 --> 00:30:54.320
slang was still in use.

00:30:54.890 --> 00:30:57.290
The freylekhs-like feeling of the song,

00:30:57.890 --> 00:30:59.900
as sung by Joseph in his testimony,

00:31:00.680 --> 00:31:02.689
becomes a vehicle for the dark humor that

00:31:02.810 --> 00:31:04.009
saturates the lyrics.

00:31:39.710 --> 00:31:41.500
From early morning on

00:31:41.740 --> 00:31:43.180
I am restless.

00:31:43.500 --> 00:31:45.500
I must immediately get moving,

00:31:45.700 --> 00:31:47.100
And I don’t know where to go.

00:31:47.500 --> 00:31:49.180
Over there in the bathroom

00:31:49.420 --> 00:31:50.980
They speak different languages.

00:31:51.340 --> 00:31:53.140
You run into someone sleeping,

00:31:53.420 --> 00:31:54.420
Just like in his own bed.

00:31:54.900 --> 00:31:58.540
Head of the barrack - you give out favors:

00:31:58.780 --> 00:32:00.900
One gets reprimanded

00:32:01.140 --> 00:32:02.500
and another one slapped

00:32:02.780 --> 00:32:04.540
Don’t start yelling,

00:32:04.780 --> 00:32:06.580
there’s nothing to eat!

00:32:06.780 --> 00:32:08.020
As father said,

00:32:08.140 --> 00:32:09.940
Everyone’s a hustler!

00:32:26.540 --> 00:32:28.380
Lunchtime comes,

00:32:28.820 --> 00:32:30.380
I turn black and blue

00:32:30.740 --> 00:32:32.260
I run here and there

00:32:32.700 --> 00:32:34.620
The Barrack Senior is yelling

00:32:34.820 --> 00:32:36.220
He wants to get some rest.

00:32:36.580 --> 00:32:38.180
They announce the chow time

00:32:38.420 --> 00:32:39.980
Everyone is rushing.

00:32:40.340 --> 00:32:42.180
One grain of porridge

00:32:42.420 --> 00:32:43.500
And an unpeeled potato

00:32:43.820 --> 00:32:47.340
Head of the barrack - you give out favors:

00:32:47.660 --> 00:32:49.780
One gets reprimanded

00:32:50.020 --> 00:32:51.620
and another one slapped

00:32:51.820 --> 00:32:53.380
Don’t start yelling,

00:32:53.540 --> 00:32:55.420
there’s nothing to eat!

00:32:55.660 --> 00:32:57.060
As father said,

00:32:57.260 --> 00:32:59.140
Everyone’s a hustler!

00:33:30.900 --> 00:33:32.140
Here, in our block,

00:33:32.780 --> 00:33:34.300
The bell has rung already.

00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:36.580
To hold your food down,

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:38.220
you shove it with a stick

00:33:38.500 --> 00:33:39.980
Oh what a fool I am,

00:33:40.420 --> 00:33:41.940
As I have no food.

00:33:42.380 --> 00:33:44.100
One starts yelling:

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:45.940
Jew, I'll give it to you!

00:33:46.300 --> 00:33:48.380
Head of the barrack

00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:50.980
you give out favors:

00:33:51.340 --> 00:33:53.220
One gets reprimanded

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:55.100
and another one slapped

00:33:55.340 --> 00:33:57.020
Don’t start yelling,

00:33:57.340 --> 00:33:58.820
there’s nothing to eat!

00:33:59.300 --> 00:34:00.420
As father said,

00:34:00.700 --> 00:34:02.580
Everyone’s a hustler!

00:34:02.980 --> 00:34:04.740
Head of the barrack

00:34:04.900 --> 00:34:06.660
you give out favors:

00:34:06.900 --> 00:34:09.020
One gets reprimanded

00:34:09.220 --> 00:34:10.540
and another one slapped

00:34:10.820 --> 00:34:12.700
Don’t start yelling,

00:34:12.940 --> 00:34:14.500
there’s nothing to eat!

00:34:14.980 --> 00:34:16.060
As father said,

00:34:16.300 --> 00:34:17.540
Everyone’s a hustler!

00:34:22.700 --> 00:34:24.420
I have worked with Craig Judelman, a truly

00:34:24.660 --> 00:34:27.179
versatile violinist, for many years

00:34:27.360 --> 00:34:29.759
in the klezmer band Litvakus, and

00:34:29.760 --> 00:34:31.709
I have been always fascinated by

00:34:32.039 --> 00:34:35.038
Craig's energy and his sound,

00:34:35.280 --> 00:34:37.350
which was exactly what I was looking for,

00:34:37.650 --> 00:34:40.139
and also his willingness to

00:34:40.140 --> 00:34:42.569
go in depth with any material that we were

00:34:43.140 --> 00:34:44.140
working with.

00:34:44.700 --> 00:34:46.469
I would like to ask Craig to share his

00:34:46.470 --> 00:34:48.830
perspective on working on Songs

00:34:48.930 --> 00:34:49.930
From Testimonies.

00:34:51.490 --> 00:34:52.490
Thanks, Zisl.

00:34:52.570 --> 00:34:54.909
But first of all, I want to thank you and

00:34:54.910 --> 00:34:56.859
all of my wonderful colleagues in the band,

00:34:57.220 --> 00:34:58.989
as well as Stephen Naron and all the people

00:34:58.990 --> 00:35:01.149
who work on the Fortunoff Archive itself,

00:35:01.510 --> 00:35:02.889
and especially the people who have the

00:35:02.890 --> 00:35:04.959
presence of mind to
record these testimonies

00:35:04.960 --> 00:35:05.960
in the first place.

00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:08.890
As Zisl has so clearly articulated,

00:35:09.100 --> 00:35:11.256
this collection of music
draws on an endless

00:35:11.350 --> 00:35:13.120
amount of sources and references,

00:35:13.750 --> 00:35:15.089
both Jewish and non.

00:35:15.310 --> 00:35:16.509
And that is certainly one of the most

00:35:16.510 --> 00:35:18.279
striking things for me when I think about

00:35:18.280 --> 00:35:19.385
this collection as a whole.

00:35:20.140 --> 00:35:22.606
However surprising
this diversity might seem,

00:35:23.050 --> 00:35:24.819
it's actually just what we should expect.

00:35:25.180 --> 00:35:27.385
If you study Yiddish
music, you quickly learn

00:35:27.700 --> 00:35:29.807
that while there's definitely a way to play

00:35:29.830 --> 00:35:32.439
with a Yiddish accent, Yiddish music,

00:35:32.440 --> 00:35:34.509
like the language itself, has always been a

00:35:34.510 --> 00:35:36.376
conglomeration in a reframing of cultures it

00:35:36.880 --> 00:35:37.880
is embedded in.

00:35:38.290 --> 00:35:40.545
So often we picture Ashkenazim in isolated

00:35:40.870 --> 00:35:43.149
shtetlekh existing in some sort

00:35:43.150 --> 00:35:44.799
of primordial, pure state.

00:35:45.250 --> 00:35:47.259
But in real life they were always in contact

00:35:47.260 --> 00:35:48.999
with their neighbors and many lived in

00:35:49.000 --> 00:35:50.919
cities, and in fact,
much of Yiddish culture

00:35:50.920 --> 00:35:52.860
was completely imbued with cosmopolitan and

00:35:53.060 --> 00:35:54.580
international influences.

00:35:55.470 --> 00:35:56.909
Part of what makes understanding and

00:35:56.910 --> 00:35:59.069
expressing Yiddish culture so difficult is

00:35:59.070 --> 00:36:00.989
the amount of contextual knowledge required

00:36:00.990 --> 00:36:02.040
to really understand it.

00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:04.709
This is challenging in any project, but I

00:36:04.710 --> 00:36:06.779
think for us, as you can see, it has been one

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:08.985
of the greatest challenges: taking a fragment

00:36:09.300 --> 00:36:11.099
of a poem or a song sung without

00:36:11.100 --> 00:36:12.913
accompaniment during an interview and

00:36:13.140 --> 00:36:14.879
searching for a way to transform it into

00:36:14.880 --> 00:36:16.709
something someone can listen to in their own

00:36:16.710 --> 00:36:18.425
living room and even enjoy it while

00:36:19.200 --> 00:36:20.969
still honoring the original meaning and

00:36:20.970 --> 00:36:22.009
context of the song.

00:36:22.410 --> 00:36:24.329
Whether it's a song about life in the camps,

00:36:24.450 --> 00:36:26.399
a popular drinking song of its day,

00:36:26.790 --> 00:36:29.099
or even an antisemitic song that somehow

00:36:29.340 --> 00:36:31.469
remained in the memory of a survivor, word

00:36:31.470 --> 00:36:32.470
for word.

00:36:33.060 --> 00:36:34.799
One might think that after the incredible

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:36.779
betrayal these survivors must have felt from

00:36:36.780 --> 00:36:38.729
their country, they would have refused to

00:36:38.730 --> 00:36:40.649
speak these languages and sing the songs of

00:36:40.650 --> 00:36:41.639
their neighbors.

00:36:41.640 --> 00:36:43.649
Yet even in these darkest moments when it

00:36:43.710 --> 00:36:45.719
must have felt clear that a person's Jewish

00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:47.669
identity would always be a barrier to their

00:36:47.670 --> 00:36:49.289
national identity in the eyes of their

00:36:49.290 --> 00:36:51.201
countrymen, the core of our culture was

00:36:51.960 --> 00:36:54.179
and remains something that is in conversation

00:36:54.180 --> 00:36:55.180
with the outside.

00:36:55.620 --> 00:36:57.779
This is, of course, true not only for Jewish

00:36:57.780 --> 00:36:59.789
or Yiddish culture, but for all cultures.

00:37:00.210 --> 00:37:01.709
And I hope that our performance of these

00:37:01.710 --> 00:37:03.509
songs helps to remind us all of that.

00:37:05.220 --> 00:37:07.179
While I've been studying and performing

00:37:07.180 --> 00:37:09.440
Yiddish music for a couple of decades now,

00:37:09.750 --> 00:37:11.759
I mostly have stayed away from explicitly

00:37:11.790 --> 00:37:13.889
Holocaust related material, preferring to

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:16.095
study, perform and celebrate the culture that

00:37:16.230 --> 00:37:18.043
lived vigorously before this travesty

00:37:18.690 --> 00:37:20.454
and is once again today only growing

00:37:20.520 --> 00:37:21.520
stronger.

00:37:22.040 --> 00:37:24.079
So often in the America I grew up in,

00:37:24.380 --> 00:37:25.819
if we did encounter things that were

00:37:25.820 --> 00:37:28.009
identifiably Yiddish, it was in the context

00:37:28.010 --> 00:37:30.169
of the Holocaust and associated with feelings

00:37:30.170 --> 00:37:32.649
of grief, victimhood and loss.

00:37:33.440 --> 00:37:35.400
I choose to engage with this culture, to

00:37:35.420 --> 00:37:37.879
reclaim my inheritance, to find life,

00:37:37.880 --> 00:37:39.840
to give context to my own experience and

00:37:40.250 --> 00:37:41.780
to honor those who came before me.

00:37:42.440 --> 00:37:44.204
However, working on this project and

00:37:44.750 --> 00:37:46.759
trying to understand the context of the songs

00:37:46.760 --> 00:37:48.889
and the people who have encountered them has

00:37:48.890 --> 00:37:51.310
been hugely enriching, if a bit

00:37:51.320 --> 00:37:52.320
challenging at times.

00:37:53.240 --> 00:37:55.189
The scope of the Holocaust makes it almost

00:37:55.190 --> 00:37:56.549
impossible to comprehend.

00:37:56.840 --> 00:37:58.849
But these songs draw us into the specific

00:37:58.850 --> 00:38:01.159
experience of individuals, and even into

00:38:01.160 --> 00:38:02.980
specific moments in their lives.

00:38:03.530 --> 00:38:05.239
I strongly encourage you all to take some

00:38:05.240 --> 00:38:07.339
time to watch the testimonies which are

00:38:07.340 --> 00:38:09.469
available on the archive website, so that

00:38:09.470 --> 00:38:11.299
when you think about this horrific chapter in

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:13.549
our history, the first image in your mind

00:38:13.550 --> 00:38:15.608
perhaps won't be mountains of dead bodies,

00:38:15.920 --> 00:38:17.899
but rather the faces of people strong enough

00:38:17.900 --> 00:38:18.900
to survive it.

00:38:19.310 --> 00:38:21.229
I'm so grateful for the way this project has

00:38:21.230 --> 00:38:22.729
been framed, and I hope that as you're

00:38:22.730 --> 00:38:24.837
watching these videos, you're able to get a

00:38:24.920 --> 00:38:27.130
sense of not of how small and confined were

00:38:27.230 --> 00:38:28.879
the experiences of the victims of this

00:38:28.880 --> 00:38:31.339
atrocity, but of how big was the world

00:38:31.340 --> 00:38:33.649
that they carried inside themselves and how

00:38:33.650 --> 00:38:35.659
interconnected it was and remains despite

00:38:36.290 --> 00:38:38.149
the continuing efforts to claim culture and

00:38:38.150 --> 00:38:40.489
land as the sole property of just a few.

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:44.890
Wow, thank you, Craig.

00:38:47.540 --> 00:38:49.160
Yeah, well, the main.

00:38:52.840 --> 00:38:54.720
Well, I guess we'll move on.

00:38:56.550 --> 00:38:59.060
The next song of Himlen, o

00:38:59.260 --> 00:39:03.860
Himlen is a powerful, chilling

00:39:04.180 --> 00:39:06.460
song recounted by Moshe

00:39:06.660 --> 00:39:09.260
F., born in Uniejów,

00:39:10.650 --> 00:39:12.810
Poland in 1913.

00:39:14.270 --> 00:39:15.838
When Moshe arrived in Auschwitz,

00:39:16.550 --> 00:39:18.280
he met a man by the name of Mayer-Ber

00:39:19.400 --> 00:39:20.400
Gutman.

00:39:21.350 --> 00:39:22.350
Or Gutman,

00:39:23.780 --> 00:39:25.729
probably, who "could write."

00:39:26.810 --> 00:39:28.480
He wrote two poems, Azoy

00:39:29.270 --> 00:39:31.639
vi ikh bin nokh Oyshvits gekimen, When

00:39:31.640 --> 00:39:33.910
I came to Auschwitz, and Himlen, o

00:39:34.010 --> 00:39:36.090
Himlen, vi iz mayn glik?

00:39:37.090 --> 00:39:38.659
Heavens, oh heavens, where is my luck?

00:39:40.000 --> 00:39:42.549
Both were sent to the melodies of popular

00:39:42.550 --> 00:39:44.417
songs of the period.

00:39:45.550 --> 00:39:46.550
The first one

00:39:47.860 --> 00:39:49.379
was originally a rejoicing song

00:39:50.200 --> 00:39:52.509
that recounts all the Jewish holidays

00:39:52.510 --> 00:39:53.649
and celebrates how

00:39:54.800 --> 00:39:57.080
it is good to be a Jew,

00:39:58.000 --> 00:39:59.925
vi git es iz zu zayn a

00:40:00.410 --> 00:40:01.410
yid.

00:40:03.410 --> 00:40:06.019
And the juxtaposition of

00:40:06.020 --> 00:40:07.588
this new text and the old melody

00:40:08.420 --> 00:40:11.119
creates a sense of tragic irony

00:40:11.120 --> 00:40:13.009
that likely would, would have been

00:40:13.820 --> 00:40:16.100
clear to the listeners of the time.

00:40:17.300 --> 00:40:18.868
The second tune and verse, which

00:40:19.640 --> 00:40:21.649
gives the title to this composition, uses

00:40:22.130 --> 00:40:24.437
the melody of a popular song,

00:40:25.490 --> 00:40:28.100
Vi iz dus gesele, vi iz di shtib.

00:40:29.380 --> 00:40:30.540
Gdie eta oolitsa,

00:40:30.740 --> 00:40:32.460
gdie etst dom in Russian or

00:40:32.660 --> 00:40:35.220
Gdzie jest ta ulica, gszie

00:40:35.380 --> 00:40:36.980
jest ten dom in Polish.

00:40:37.180 --> 00:40:39.300
Where is that street, where is that house.

00:40:40.140 --> 00:40:41.860
It was a very, very popular

00:40:42.020 --> 00:40:42.900
song.

00:40:47.540 --> 00:40:49.100
When we arrived

00:40:49.340 --> 00:40:50.820
in Auschwitz,

00:40:51.300 --> 00:40:53.060
They took away the women

00:40:53.380 --> 00:40:54.900
and the children.

00:40:55.220 --> 00:40:57.020
A great tumult

00:40:57.340 --> 00:40:59.410
happened there:

00:40:59.810 --> 00:41:01.500
"In half an hour

00:41:01.740 --> 00:41:04.220
we will be in heaven."

00:42:23.620 --> 00:42:28.060
When we arrived in Auschwitz,

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:32.860
They took away the women and the children.

00:42:33.020 --> 00:42:37.740
A great tumult happened there:

00:42:38.020 --> 00:42:44.180
In half an hour we will be in heaven.

00:42:44.420 --> 00:42:48.620
At night, on our prison beds

00:42:48.860 --> 00:42:52.460
We lay our skinny bones

00:42:52.780 --> 00:42:56.340
And sleep with hope in our hearts

00:42:56.540 --> 00:43:00.660
That soon we will be free.

00:43:00.860 --> 00:43:04.260
At night, on our prison beds

00:43:04.740 --> 00:43:08.260
We lay our skinny bones

00:43:08.460 --> 00:43:12.180
And sleep with hope in our hearts

00:43:12.340 --> 00:43:16.060
That soon we will be free.

00:44:01.180 --> 00:44:04.500
Heavens, oh heavens,

00:44:04.980 --> 00:44:08.300
where is my luck?

00:44:08.700 --> 00:44:13.740
The moon and the snow

00:44:14.020 --> 00:44:16.780
are hidden from sight.

00:44:17.340 --> 00:44:20.580
Where are our children?

00:44:20.860 --> 00:44:24.060
In what land?

00:44:24.420 --> 00:44:27.660
In Auschwitz, in Treblinka,

00:44:28.340 --> 00:44:31.140
torn apart and disgraced.

00:44:32.060 --> 00:44:35.300
Where are our children?

00:44:35.860 --> 00:44:38.980
In what land?

00:44:39.540 --> 00:44:42.580
In Auschwitz, in Treblinka,

00:44:43.140 --> 00:44:46.300
torn apart and disgraced.

00:45:31.700 --> 00:45:35.380
Heavens, oh heavens,

00:45:36.180 --> 00:45:41.260
where is my luck?

00:45:47.220 --> 00:45:49.490
I've had an honor of working with

00:45:49.500 --> 00:45:51.670
the bassist Dmitry Ishenko

00:45:51.780 --> 00:45:53.850
on a wide range of projects

00:45:54.120 --> 00:45:56.227
from klezmer band, here the substitute bass

00:45:56.460 --> 00:45:58.830
player in Litvakus band, to

00:45:58.860 --> 00:46:00.210
improvised music.

00:46:00.460 --> 00:46:02.330
Dmitry plays in Zisl Slepovitch

00:46:02.760 --> 00:46:04.580
Trio, along with the wonderful guitarist

00:46:04.690 --> 00:46:07.109
Nadav Lev, to musical theater,

00:46:07.920 --> 00:46:09.899
Fiddler on the Roof in Yiddish being one of

00:46:09.900 --> 00:46:11.870
the more recent productions and more,

00:46:12.330 --> 00:46:14.642
it is truly my pleasure to ask Dmitry

00:46:14.790 --> 00:46:17.069
to share his story of working on songs from

00:46:17.070 --> 00:46:18.070
testimonies.

00:46:19.170 --> 00:46:20.170
Thank you so much, Zisl.

00:46:21.540 --> 00:46:23.647
Well, it has been a real, true honor for me

00:46:23.730 --> 00:46:26.099
to be part of this project, and

00:46:26.460 --> 00:46:28.499
it really has exposed me to a whole wide

00:46:28.500 --> 00:46:30.068
variety of music and a multitude

00:46:30.810 --> 00:46:32.879
of languages that comes out of the Holocaust

00:46:32.880 --> 00:46:35.249
era and not all of it

00:46:35.520 --> 00:46:37.529
being sad, some of it being quite hopeful

00:46:37.830 --> 00:46:38.830
and optimistic.

00:46:39.800 --> 00:46:42.469
And from a personal perspective,

00:46:43.280 --> 00:46:45.379
for me, it's a dedication to the Jewish side

00:46:45.380 --> 00:46:47.689
of my family that hails from

00:46:48.230 --> 00:46:50.779
Odessa, Ukraine, and

00:46:51.440 --> 00:46:52.670
the surrounding areas,

00:46:53.930 --> 00:46:55.660
and their struggles during that era,

00:46:56.810 --> 00:46:59.089
and to the several relatives that I know

00:46:59.090 --> 00:47:01.040
of who have perished during that time

00:47:01.550 --> 00:47:03.719
at the hands of the Nazi allies.

00:47:04.490 --> 00:47:05.490
Thank you.

00:47:07.660 --> 00:47:08.660
Thank you, Dmitry.

00:47:09.840 --> 00:47:11.990
This next song, Hej Tam Na Górce,

00:47:12.780 --> 00:47:15.359
Hey There on the Hill, was drawn

00:47:15.360 --> 00:47:17.310
from the testimony of Yaakov B.

00:47:18.330 --> 00:47:20.500
Yaakov was born in Hrubieszów, Poland

00:47:21.030 --> 00:47:23.020
in 1926, the youngest of

00:47:23.220 --> 00:47:24.140
three children.

00:47:24.870 --> 00:47:27.179
He first attended a Jewish school but

00:47:27.180 --> 00:47:29.091
then underwent a compulsory transfer to

00:47:29.580 --> 00:47:31.019
the public Polish school.

00:47:31.680 --> 00:47:34.049
From those years, he remembers walking

00:47:34.050 --> 00:47:36.479
around the town with the girls and

00:47:36.480 --> 00:47:39.059
learning funny and frivolous songs from them.

00:47:39.450 --> 00:47:41.789
Hej Tam Na Górce is a traditional

00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:44.429
Polish song with a pronounced

00:47:44.580 --> 00:47:45.990
3/4 dance feel.

00:47:46.590 --> 00:47:48.256
This inspires us to pair, inspired

00:47:48.990 --> 00:47:50.540
us to pair the song with the oberek

00:47:51.450 --> 00:47:53.336
dance tune from the microregion

00:47:54.120 --> 00:47:56.423
Rezyczyca, a town near Yaakov's

00:47:56.610 --> 00:47:57.610
hometown of Hrubieszów.

00:47:59.860 --> 00:48:02.499
The song belongs to the group of

00:48:02.800 --> 00:48:05.139
light hearted Polish songs

00:48:05.140 --> 00:48:06.953
about a soldier flirting with a young

00:48:07.420 --> 00:48:08.420
girl.

00:48:08.530 --> 00:48:10.157
We suspect that Yaakov

00:48:10.990 --> 00:48:13.419
B. might have added his own

00:48:13.420 --> 00:48:15.420
name to the song lyrics.

00:48:16.060 --> 00:48:17.971
It's impossible to know if the original

00:48:18.670 --> 00:48:19.944
version of the song is the

00:48:20.950 --> 00:48:22.920
Jewish name, Jakow, or Jankiel,

00:48:25.050 --> 00:48:27.899
or the Polish name Jakub.

00:48:32.870 --> 00:48:35.220
Over there on the hill,

00:48:35.420 --> 00:48:36.820
by the gate,

00:48:37.140 --> 00:48:38.660
Over there on the hill,

00:48:38.860 --> 00:48:40.220
by the gate, by the gate,

00:48:40.460 --> 00:48:42.180
Yakov is standing there

