00:52:24.220 --> 00:52:26.300
This next piece, Chlopek-Roztropek,

00:52:26.780 --> 00:52:29.179
is composed of two songs from two

00:52:29.180 --> 00:52:30.300
different testimonies.

00:52:30.650 --> 00:52:33.110
One is the satirical Polish song

00:52:33.800 --> 00:52:35.749
Chlopek-Roztropek, Is a Village Hustler,

00:52:36.380 --> 00:52:38.955
overheard in the streets
of Warsaw by Zalman

00:52:39.200 --> 00:52:41.359
H., then a little boy,

00:52:41.690 --> 00:52:43.000
born in 1929.

00:52:44.240 --> 00:52:46.699
The song's general concept is poking

00:52:46.700 --> 00:52:48.860
fun and at uneducated,

00:52:49.040 --> 00:52:50.300
yet street smart peasants.

00:52:50.930 --> 00:52:53.090
Zalman did not remember

00:52:53.270 --> 00:52:55.609
one of the lines that we're able to

00:52:55.610 --> 00:52:56.835
discover in a source that

00:52:58.010 --> 00:52:59.099
contained the full text.

00:52:59.930 --> 00:53:01.743
As the character is ironically making

00:53:02.300 --> 00:53:04.789
fun of his girlfriend, pointing out that

00:53:04.790 --> 00:53:07.189
she dresses too fancy, she fires

00:53:07.190 --> 00:53:08.767
back, noting that her Wojtek

00:53:09.860 --> 00:53:12.499
bought a fur coat from
the Jew in the ghetto.

00:53:13.100 --> 00:53:15.199
It was obviously a
cheap coat, and he didn't

00:53:15.200 --> 00:53:17.389
see anything particularly immoral

00:53:17.750 --> 00:53:18.859
about this purchase.

00:53:19.460 --> 00:53:21.679
In my arrangement, I have cut the song's

00:53:21.680 --> 00:53:23.630
lyric at that dramatic turn

00:53:23.990 --> 00:53:25.656
and transition to the next picture

00:53:26.540 --> 00:53:28.669
from exhibition, so to speak, from a

00:53:28.670 --> 00:53:30.889
testimony given by Kalman A.

00:53:38.349 --> 00:53:41.139
Kalman was born in 1928,

00:53:41.740 --> 00:53:44.260
a native of Kaunas, Lithuania.

00:53:44.740 --> 00:53:47.289
He resemble, he remembers,

00:53:47.440 --> 00:53:48.910
one verse of a song portraying

00:53:49.900 --> 00:53:53.249
a strict Polish collaborationist

00:53:54.430 --> 00:53:57.130
who is guarding the entrance of the ghetto.

00:53:57.490 --> 00:53:59.559
He suddenly becomes happy

00:53:59.950 --> 00:54:02.349
when he sees an opportunity

00:54:02.350 --> 00:54:04.689
to get a cut of what is

00:54:04.690 --> 00:54:06.013
likely food being illegally

00:54:07.210 --> 00:54:08.719
smuggled into the ghetto.

00:54:09.970 --> 00:54:11.342
The characters of both songs

00:54:12.340 --> 00:54:13.340
represent the same

00:54:14.620 --> 00:54:16.241
figure: a non-Jew,

00:54:16.930 --> 00:54:17.930
former neighbor, who

00:54:19.740 --> 00:54:21.960
under new circumstances,

00:54:22.200 --> 00:54:25.229
doesn't mind benefiting personally

00:54:25.230 --> 00:54:26.780
from the misery of Jews,

00:54:27.570 --> 00:54:30.179
the moral opposite of those

00:54:30.390 --> 00:54:33.059
who risked their lives by hiding

00:54:33.060 --> 00:54:35.219
their Jewish neighbors from the Nazis.

00:55:04.980 --> 00:55:07.980
I am a village hustler, I live in a shack,

00:55:08.220 --> 00:55:11.260
Life here is not
like it is in silly Warsaw,

00:55:11.540 --> 00:55:14.500
We peasants have a
good life in the village,

00:55:14.780 --> 00:55:18.180
And the prices in Warsaw
will starve you soon.

00:55:18.620 --> 00:55:21.620
You took the furniture
and clothes out of Warsaw,

00:55:21.860 --> 00:55:24.860
So, we don’t have anything left to sell.

00:55:25.220 --> 00:55:28.660
We are keeping your
pianos in the stables,

00:55:28.900 --> 00:55:31.700
Because we don’t have
enough space for that

00:55:58.780 --> 00:56:02.020
You called us "foolish rednecks"

00:56:02.380 --> 00:56:05.380
And I say, we don’t have fish for brains!

00:56:05.660 --> 00:56:08.860
When you come to the
village to shop,

00:56:09.100 --> 00:56:12.540
You'll be counting out
how much you owe for days!

00:57:05.700 --> 00:57:08.540
We fancy people are not going anywhere,

00:57:08.820 --> 00:57:12.180
But we will overcharge you for some lard.

00:57:12.380 --> 00:57:15.300
They took everything
from you on those trains,

00:57:15.620 --> 00:57:18.540
While we stuffed
our mattresses with cash!

00:57:19.500 --> 00:57:22.180
I thought to myself, these are great times

00:57:22.420 --> 00:57:25.580
When I saw Kasia wearing silk underwear.

00:57:26.020 --> 00:57:28.860
Don’t laugh, my Wojtek,
you’re not any better,

00:57:29.540 --> 00:57:32.700
You recently got yourself
a fur coat in the ghetto!

00:57:47.060 --> 00:57:48.900
A peasant stands at the ghetto gate,

00:57:49.100 --> 00:57:50.700
With a sour face.

00:57:50.900 --> 00:57:52.300
He guards the ghetto like a bank,

00:57:52.620 --> 00:57:54.340
With a rifle in his hand.

00:57:54.580 --> 00:57:56.100
But suddenly he is happy,

00:57:56.340 --> 00:57:57.860
And feels like a king.

00:57:58.100 --> 00:57:59.740
A brigade is coming,

00:57:59.900 --> 00:58:01.220
Fully stocked.

00:58:01.420 --> 00:58:03.300
A peasant stands at the ghetto gate,

00:58:03.500 --> 00:58:04.980
With a sour face.

00:58:05.220 --> 00:58:06.740
He guards the ghetto like a bank,

00:58:06.900 --> 00:58:08.340
With a rifle in his hand.

00:58:08.500 --> 00:58:09.980
But suddenly he is happy,

00:58:10.300 --> 00:58:11.580
And feels like a king.

00:58:11.820 --> 00:58:13.580
A brigade is coming,

00:58:13.780 --> 00:58:15.020
Fully stocked.

00:58:37.500 --> 00:58:38.740
The last song

00:58:39.780 --> 00:58:42.460
in our tonight's concert is

00:58:42.660 --> 00:58:44.620
Shray, Hertsale, Shray?

00:58:45.420 --> 00:58:46.780
Cry, My Heart,

00:58:47.020 --> 00:58:48.020
Cry.

00:58:48.590 --> 00:58:50.930
The title track on the second volume

00:58:51.080 --> 00:58:53.930
of Songs From Testimonies.

00:58:55.010 --> 00:58:56.431
In interwar Europe, including

00:58:57.680 --> 00:58:59.540
the Soviet Union, Latin

00:58:59.740 --> 00:59:02.139
American music was immensely

00:59:02.140 --> 00:59:03.140
popular.

00:59:05.070 --> 00:59:07.340
It is no surprise that some songs

00:59:07.620 --> 00:59:10.139
of survival and resistance during

00:59:10.170 --> 00:59:12.689
and after the war adapted

00:59:13.170 --> 00:59:15.779
popular Latin American tunes

00:59:16.410 --> 00:59:19.049
to new lyrics and became

00:59:19.680 --> 00:59:21.720
widespread as new pieces.

00:59:22.870 --> 00:59:24.860
The musical origins of the

00:59:25.020 --> 00:59:27.509
so-called Treblinka Song

00:59:28.170 --> 00:59:30.340
lie in Tango Oracíon,

00:59:30.660 --> 00:59:32.473
composed by the prominent Argentinian

00:59:33.030 --> 00:59:35.489
composer and tango orchestra

00:59:35.490 --> 00:59:37.289
director Eduardo Bianco.

00:59:39.420 --> 00:59:42.059
While the Treblinka song is based

00:59:42.060 --> 00:59:44.789
on Oracíon, it has its own

00:59:45.030 --> 00:59:47.489
distinct feel,

00:59:47.730 --> 00:59:49.440
tempo and form.

00:59:51.240 --> 00:59:54.179
We find this song in more than one testimony,

00:59:54.690 --> 00:59:55.690
both in the Fortunoff--two

00:59:57.600 --> 00:59:59.730
testimonies in the Fortunoff Video Archive

00:59:59.970 --> 01:00:02.390
and in the United States Holocaust Memorial

01:00:02.400 --> 01:00:03.420
Museum collection.

01:00:03.990 --> 01:00:06.420
It was sung by Morris K., which

01:00:06.620 --> 01:00:08.449
we-- that version we use as the main

01:00:08.450 --> 01:00:10.568
reference for this recording, Willy

01:00:10.820 --> 01:00:13.550
F., who presents a short fragment

01:00:13.760 --> 01:00:16.140
from the song, and Frieda Bursztyn

01:00:16.380 --> 01:00:18.340
Radasky, who sang a fuller

01:00:18.540 --> 01:00:20.789
but also slightly different version titled

01:00:21.000 --> 01:00:23.519
Treblinka Dort, There Lies

01:00:23.520 --> 01:00:24.520
Treblinka.

01:00:25.110 --> 01:00:27.940
The song conveys the story of frequent

01:00:28.140 --> 01:00:30.900
deputations from Warsaw's Umschlagplatz

01:00:31.380 --> 01:00:33.740
to Treblinka as seen and felt

01:00:33.940 --> 01:00:35.220
by the ghetto prisoners.

01:00:35.560 --> 01:00:38.649
Morris K., born in 1917,

01:00:38.920 --> 01:00:40.869
heard the song while he was still living with

01:00:40.870 --> 01:00:43.380
his family in his hometown, Polaniec,

01:00:43.960 --> 01:00:45.773
situated more than two hundred thirty

01:00:45.850 --> 01:00:48.580
kilometers, or one hundred and forty miles

01:00:48.700 --> 01:00:50.620
south of Warsaw, before

01:00:50.860 --> 01:00:53.739
they were ordered to leave in October 1942.

01:00:54.550 --> 01:00:56.860
Another survivor, Willy F.,

01:00:57.160 --> 01:00:59.460
born in Dzialoszyce in

01:00:59.660 --> 01:01:02.060
1928, lived through

01:01:02.960 --> 01:01:05.420
the Kraków ghetto, Plaszów,

01:01:05.780 --> 01:01:08.020
Skarzysko-Kamienna, and Buchenwald

01:01:08.420 --> 01:01:10.060
concentration camps.

01:01:10.340 --> 01:01:12.830
He learned the song in Kraków-Plaszów area,

01:01:13.100 --> 01:01:15.620
which is even farther away from Warsaw.

01:01:38.890 --> 01:01:46.620
A storm raged through the world,

01:01:47.140 --> 01:01:51.740
Entire nations remained
without land

01:01:53.260 --> 01:01:55.820
Without mercy

01:01:56.140 --> 01:01:59.820
the storm destroyed the world

01:02:01.260 --> 01:02:04.100
It ripped the sun out of the sky

01:02:04.820 --> 01:02:09.740
and turned day to night.

01:02:13.900 --> 01:02:15.900
There, not far,

01:02:16.660 --> 01:02:19.700
There is a deportation site

01:02:21.300 --> 01:02:24.940
Where people are shoved

01:02:26.060 --> 01:02:29.820
To the cattle cars.

01:02:30.820 --> 01:02:34.500
There one hears a scream,

01:02:34.940 --> 01:02:38.740
A child calling for his mother:

01:02:39.340 --> 01:02:43.140
‘How come you’re leaving me alone?

01:02:43.820 --> 01:02:47.540
Let's go together!’

01:02:49.340 --> 01:02:51.820
The police,

01:02:52.420 --> 01:02:55.860
They ordered them to move faster.

01:02:56.380 --> 01:03:00.620
You will not suffer any hardship

01:03:01.100 --> 01:03:05.860
You will get three pieces of bread.

01:03:06.220 --> 01:03:09.660
They didn’t know,

01:03:10.140 --> 01:03:14.140
They are going to their deaths.

01:03:15.420 --> 01:03:18.180
Over there, in Treblinka,

01:03:19.020 --> 01:03:24.100
A fitting place for every Jew.

01:03:27.020 --> 01:03:29.220
I’m here, you’re there,

01:03:30.220 --> 01:03:34.220
You can’t share another word.

01:03:34.820 --> 01:03:38.260
Those who come there, stay there,

01:03:39.020 --> 01:03:43.140
There, in Treblinka.

01:03:43.700 --> 01:03:47.460
The heart pains

01:03:47.980 --> 01:03:52.100
When one starts to remember

01:03:53.420 --> 01:03:56.540
Father and mother

01:03:56.860 --> 01:04:00.860
on the way to Treblinka.

01:04:02.980 --> 01:04:06.220
Cry, my heart, cry,

01:04:06.540 --> 01:04:09.780
My heart, don’t just say,

01:04:10.340 --> 01:04:13.020
That you a Jew.

01:04:16.380 --> 01:04:18.500
Sisters, brothers,

01:04:18.700 --> 01:04:20.500
dear parents

01:04:20.860 --> 01:04:24.860
They are all going together,

01:04:25.060 --> 01:04:29.100
They all are going to the poisoning,

01:04:29.380 --> 01:04:33.220
Because you are a Jew!

01:04:33.940 --> 01:04:36.540
Cry, my heart, cry,

01:04:37.060 --> 01:04:40.620
My heart, don't just say

01:04:41.260 --> 01:04:43.900
That you are a Jew!

01:05:03.700 --> 01:05:06.500
Cry, my heart, cry,

01:05:06.940 --> 01:05:10.300
My heart, don’t just say,

01:05:10.660 --> 01:05:13.860
That you a Jew.

01:05:16.540 --> 01:05:18.620
Sisters, brothers,

01:05:18.860 --> 01:05:20.580
